Author:     Sam Ruby <[redacted-email]>
AuthorDate: Wed Oct 21 10:27:27 2026 -0400
Commit:     Sam Ruby <[redacted-email]>
CommitDate: Wed Oct 21 10:27:27 2026 -0400

    current_transaction.after_commit runs its block once the transaction commits
    
    Rails 7.2's `Model.current_transaction.after_commit { ... }` runs the
    block after the open transaction commits, never for one that rolls back,
    and at once where no transaction can hold it (NullTransaction, which is
    also what an unjoinable transaction answers, as a transactional test's
    does). Campfire's password change closes the user's live connections
    this way since once-campfire c8f74a0; it was "no known method
    current_transaction on User".
    
    Runtime: `ActiveRecord::Transaction#after_commit(work)` and
    `Base._after_commit` hold the work on `Db._txn_callbacks` (thread/fiber
    local in every Db shim, as `_txn_records` is); the outermost COMMIT runs
    it after the records' commit callbacks, a savepoint that runs its own
    callbacks runs its own, and a ROLLBACK (to a savepoint, too) drops it.
    The work is a lambda, `^() -> nil`, as ActiveJob's queue holds work:
    lower::transaction_after_commit turns the app's block into one, so the
    runtime types fully on every lane. Only `after_commit` on a
    `current_transaction` receiver is modeled; anything else on the handle
    stays the analyzer's error.
    
    Ledgers: the RBS-paired gate stays at 860 (`Db._txn_callbacks` is typed
    beside `_txn_records`); the RBS-less probe 1043 -> 1082 for the reads of
    the held list.
    
    tests/emit_and_run.rs current_transaction_after_commit_waits_for_the_
    commit: held until the enclosing COMMIT, dropped on rollback, run after
    a save's own commit, run at once with no transaction (the hold and
    rollback cases fail when the work always runs at once).
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <[redacted-email]>

